In a category \( \mathfrak{A} \), consider the following commutative diagram of morphisms:
\[
\begin{CD}
A @>{f}>> B\\
@V{h}VV @V{k}VV\\
C @>{g}>> D
\end{CD}
\]
Assume that \( k \circ f = g \circ h \) and there exists a unique morphism \( w: B \to C \) such that \( w \circ f = h \). Can it be deduced that \( g \circ w = k \)?

Find all functions \( f: Q \to Q \) such that:
1. \( f(1) = 2 \);
2. For any \( x, y \in Q \), \( f(xy) = f(x) f(y) - f(x + y) + 1 \).
